    {
      "path": "app/src/main/java/tv/own/owntv/kevbox/errors/ErrorQueue.kt",
      "content": "`ErrorEntry.proxy` is `Boolean?`, and `ErrorReporter` sets it to null when the proxy read throws (`guardedOrNull { proxyUsable() }`). As written, a newer repeat whose read failed replaces a known `true`/`false` with null, so the merged entry loses the flag this fix is meant to keep. Only take the newer value when it is non-null.",
      "suggestion_code": "                proxy = if (latest) entry.proxy ?: target.entry.proxy else target.entry.proxy ?: entry.proxy,",
      "existing_code": "                proxy = if (latest) entry.proxy else target.entry.proxy,",
      "start_line": 70,
      "end_line": 70,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/src/main/java/tv/own/owntv/kevbox/usage/PlayNotes.kt",
      "content": "The app scope from AppModule is a `SupervisorJob` with no `CoroutineExceptionHandler`. On Android, any uncaught exception in these `launch` blocks therefore crashes the process. In `played()`, only the network call is wrapped. `zone()` (`ZoneId.systemDefault()` can throw `ZoneRulesException`/`DateTimeException`), `playedAtText` and `now()` all run outside the try. PlayWatcher has a `guarded` wrapper, but PlayNotes does not. Wrap the bodies of `start`, `onPlayed` and `onForeground` in the same guard (rethrow `CancellationException`, swallow the rest), so a telemetry feature can never take down playback.",
      "suggestion_code": "    fun onPlayed() {\n        scope.launch(Dispatchers.IO) { guarded { played() } }\n    }\n\n    private suspend fun guarded(block: suspend () -\u003e Unit) {\n        try { block() } catch (e: CancellationException) { throw e } catch (_: Exception) { }\n    }",
      "existing_code": "    fun onPlayed() {\n        scope.launch(Dispatchers.IO) { played() }\n    }",
      "start_line": 97,
      "end_line": 99,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/src/main/java/tv/own/owntv/kevbox/usage/PlayWatcher.kt",
      "content": "`ErrorReporter` calls its listeners for every queued entry, including `Failure.Guide` (EPG feed) and `Failure.Playlist` entries, not only playback failures. A guide-feed error on a TV that plays fine will lift the hourly limit, so the next 30-second play sends an extra note. If the server's incident logic only cares about playback failures, filter here, for example `addFailureListener { if (it.engine != null) onFailureRecorded() }`. Otherwise, document that every entry kind counts.",
      "suggestion_code": "        addFailureListener { entry -\u003e if (entry.engine != null) onFailureRecorded() }",
      "existing_code": "        addFailureListener { onFailureRecorded() }",
      "start_line": 146,
      "end_line": 146,
      "category": "bug",
      "severity": "low"
    },
    {
      "path": "app/src/main/java/tv/own/owntv/kevbox/usage/PlayNotes.kt",
      "content": "`lastSentAtMs` is only set on a successful send. While the network is down, every new 30-second run (for example after each channel change) builds a note and tries to send at once, outside the 15-minute tick and app-open retries. The same happens while a failure stays uncleared. It is not a tight loop, but it contradicts the class doc. When a note is already pending and the send fails, consider replacing the pending note's content without sending it right away.",
      "existing_code": "        if (!playNoteDue(last, failureSinceLastSent = failuresNow != failuresCleared, nowMs = at)) return@withLock",
      "start_line": 119,
      "end_line": 119,
      "category": "other",
      "severity": "low"
    }
  ],
